(a) The State Board of Education shall establish common minimum facility requirements which each public school facility shall meet in order to be certified for use in any component of the educational or recreational program of such school. Such minimum requirements shall:
(1) Include such provisions of law or state board policy on matters that relate to:
(A) Fire and physical safety;
(B) Sanitation and health, including temperature and ventilation;
(C) Minimum space, size, and configuration for the various components of the instructional program; and
(D) Construction stability, quality, and suitability for intended uses;
(2) Not prohibit wood construction that is otherwise in compliance with state minimum standard codes as they existed on January 1, 2014; and
(3) Provide that for each new elementary school constructed on or after July 1, 2027, which includes a playground, such playground shall meet the following design standards:
(A) Provides a sensory-diverse environment that enables children of all abilities to develop physically, socially, and emotionally;
(B) Provides an engaging play space with age and ability-appropriate levels of challenge and offers opportunities for children of all abilities to succeed, regardless of physical and intellectual attributes;
(C) Enables all students to benefit from state mandated recess time for elementary students;
(D) Capable for use as an outdoor classroom that is open to all students; and
(E) Meets the following minimum guidelines:
(i) Playground is accessible via a sidewalk or pathway from the school;
(ii) Playground is a minimum of 10,000 square feet in area;
(iii) Fencing surrounds and encloses the entire playground area;
(iv) A minimum of 20 percent of the total playground area shall be shaded; provided, however, that such shaded area may comprise more than one area so long as the total shaded area meets or exceeds 20 percent of the total playground area; and, provided, further, that such shading may be provided by natural means, such as existing mature trees, or man-made means, such as sun canopies;
(v) Unitary surfacing shall be used throughout at least 80 percent of the playground area. Examples of unitary surfacing include, but are not limited to, turf, bonded rubber, and poured in place rubber; and
(vi) Multiple components that are installed on a portion of the playground with the unitary surfacing and are accessible for all users that address the physical, sensory, cognitive, social, emotional, imaginative, and communication needs of those who will visit the playground, such as:
(I) Communication boards;
(II) Learning American Sign Language (ASL) alphabet panels;
(III) Learning Braille alphabet panels;
(IV) Roller slides;
(V) Pieces of equipment that make music, such as chimes, drums, and bells;
(VI) Adaptive or accessible swings;
(VII) Play pieces that are accessible to a child remaining in a wheelchair that allows such child to play with other children;
(VIII) Sensory panels;
(IX) Wheelchair accessible raised structures; and
(X) Wheelchair accessible slides.

20-2-270.3.text:
(a) The board of control of each regional educational service agency (RESA) shall authorize regional literacy coaches and regional leadership literacy coaches to provide literacy coaching, support, and related services to local school systems and other public schools in the RESA service area.
(b) In collaboration with the State Literacy Director and the Georgia Literacy Coach Coordinator, the board of control of each RESA shall assign regional literacy coaches to the local school systems and public schools of the RESA service area, and at least one regional leadership literacy coach shall be assigned to serve as the primary point of contact with local school system and school leaders.
(c) Each regional literacy coach and regional leadership literacy coach authorized by the board of control of a RESA shall be employed by such RESA and shall report to the director of the RESA.
(d) Each regional literacy coach, regional leadership literacy coach, and RESA director shall work in full cooperation with the Georgia Literacy Coordinating Committee, the State Literacy Director, and the Georgia Literacy Coach Coordinator.
(e) Each regional literacy coach and regional leadership literacy coach shall work in full cooperation with any observation, evaluation, and feedback provided by the Georgia Literacy Coach Coordinator or his or her designee.
(f) (1) The State Board of Education, in collaboration with the Office of Student Achievement, shall establish the minimum qualifications and duties for regional literacy coaches.
(2) The minimum qualifications for regional literacy coaches shall include, but shall not be limited to:
(A) Certification by the Professional Standards Commission;
(B) A minimum of five years of documented successful classroom teaching or literacy intervention experience at assigned grade levels; and
(C) A knowledge of scientifically based reading research, special expertise in quality reading instruction and intervention, dyslexia specific interventions, and data analysis.
(3) The minimum duties for regional literacy coaches shall include, but shall not be limited to:
(A) Coaching educators using nonevaluative, evidence based practices, including modeling instruction, analyzing instructional practice and data, providing actionable feedback, and delivering side-by-side classroom support;
(B) Collaborating with school principals, school based literacy coaches, and representatives of the Department of Education and the Office of Student Achievement;
(C) Providing ongoing support to local school systems and public schools for effective implementation and continuous improvement of comprehensive literacy programs;
(D) Providing ongoing professional learning activities and materials for teachers and literacy coaches that include research on the science of reading and curriculum based professional learning to support them in applying such training and research into instructional practices; and
(E) Analyzing student data, including screening, diagnostic, and progress monitoring data, to inform instruction, grouping, and intervention within a multi-tiered system of supports framework.
(g) (1) The State Board of Education, in collaboration with the Office of Student Achievement, shall establish the minimum qualifications and duties for regional leadership literacy coaches.
(2) The minimum qualifications for regional leadership literacy coaches shall include, but shall not be limited to:
(A) Certification by the Professional Standards Commission;
(B) A minimum of five years of documented success as a literacy coach or as a classroom teacher or administrator in kindergarten to grade three;
(C) A knowledge of scientifically based reading research, special expertise in quality reading instruction and intervention, dyslexia specific interventions, and data analysis;
(D) Successful experience in leading a school, a school district, or a regional literacy program to include the role of principal, curriculum director, superintendent, or state-level literacy specialist;
(E) Extensive knowledge of the implementation of a structured literacy program that is founded in the science of reading;
(F) Successful experience in working with adult learners;
(G) Exceptional interpersonal skills;
(H) Exceptional communication skills; and
(I) Successful experience in leading educational programs and/or initiatives.
(3) The minimum duties for regional leadership literacy coaches shall include, but shall not be limited to:
(A) Attending and participating in professional learning related to literacy improvement and coaching within the RESA network;
(B) Collaborating with local school superintendents and school principals to create a literacy rich environment within the local school system, with priority given to schools that include kindergarten through third grade;
(C) Joining local school superintendents and school principals in side-by-side classroom observations to support teachers and paraprofessionals in applying the science of reading into instructional practices;
(D) Assisting regional literacy coaches to prioritize the needs of the local school system based on student data and communication with local school system leaders;
(E) Facilitating communication and collaboration between the local school system and regional literacy coaches;
(F) Providing guidance to local school systems in the development and implementation of the local unified literacy plan;
(G) Facilitating collaboration between the local school system and the local community to support literacy development for children from birth to age five;
(H) Facilitating collaboration between the local school system and the local and regional postsecondary educational institutions; and
(I) Collaborating with other regional leadership literacy coaches. SECTION 1-4. Said title is further amended in Subpart 1 of Part 6 of Article 6 of Chapter 2, relating to certificated professional personnel, by adding a new Code section to read as follows:

